WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.337
Project B focuses on time grain.

00:00:02.337 --> 00:00:05.177
The source table has one row per order event.

00:00:05.177 --> 00:00:09.372
We truncate the timestamp to month, then group at that new grain.

00:00:09.372 --> 00:00:15.762
The output contains monthly order count, distinct customers, revenue, and average order value.

00:00:15.762 --> 00:00:20.527
This is the kind of feature table commonly used for dashboards or later modeling.

00:00:20.527 --> 00:00:25.577
Connect this idea to an operations team aligning timestamps across locations.

00:00:25.577 --> 00:00:30.512
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:30.512 --> 00:00:37.182
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:37.182 --> 00:00:42.297
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:42.297 --> 00:00:47.520
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
